        "og:image": "/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png",
        "og:image:secure_url": "/static/browse/0.3.4/images/arxiv-logo-fb.png",
        "og:image:width": "1200",
        "og:image:height": "700",
        "og:image:alt": "arXiv logo",
        "og:description": "According to Dennett, the same system may be described using a `physical' (mechanical) explanatory stance, or using an `intentional' (belief- and goal-based) explanatory stance. Humans tend to find the physical stance more helpful for certain systems, such as planets orbiting a star, and the intentional stance for others, such as living animals. We define a formal counterpart of physical and intentional stances within computational theory: a description of a system as either a device, or an agent, with the key difference being that `devices' are directly described in terms of an input-output mapping, while `agents' are described in terms of the function they optimise. Bayes' rule can then be applied to calculate the subjective probability of a system being a device or an agent, based only on its behaviour. We illustrate this using the trajectories of an object in a toy grid-world domain.",
